 "amendments": [
  "T10 is placed in a second module lean/G12AHilbertAdjoint.lean that imports lean/G12AContinuumAdjoint.lean unchanged (FROZEN_TARGETS.md names only G12AContinuumAdjoint as the module); the T10 signatures are exactly the frozen ones. FROZEN_TARGETS.md itself is not edited.",
  "raw/run_compile.py gained a 'hilbert' step on resume (no other change).",
  "Helper declarations not listed in FROZEN_TARGETS.md (amgm_abs, ac_of_pos, wsq_integrable, gain_memLp, integral_count_bool, integral_count_bool2, sgnB, weight_measurable, weight_pos, nu_ae_pos, instances energyMeasure_sFinite/dOmega_isFinite; in the T10 module inner_L2W, norm_sq_L2W, gain_congr_ae, collOp_congr_ae, section_integrable, gain_sq_integral_le, gain_toLp_norm_le, collLp, collLp_coeFn, collLp_add, collLp_smul, collLp_norm_le, collLin and the extras listed under T10) are auxiliary; they are audited but claim nothing beyond their types."
 ],
 "scope_and_caveats": [
  "Layer 1 (T4, T6) keeps the source's conditional hypotheses explicit: L*K_j in L2(W dnu) (hLK) plus kernel-Fubini integrability (hG, hloss).",
  "Layer 2 (T5) and T10 hypotheses are sufficient conditions: W = w(E) energy-only, measurable, >0 a.e.; |tau| <= tau_max everywhere; P jointly measurable with |P| <= P_max; sigma finite; rho s-finite. Symmetry/normalization of P are not assumed except in T9.",
  "T7 takes the angular eigen-relation int P(e',e) K(E,e') dsigma(e') = p K(E,e) as a hypothesis; the Legendre eigenvalues p_0=1, p_2=1/10, p_l=0 and kernel normalization are not derived here (G12-B/CAS12-C02).",
  "T10's Hilbert adjoint lives on L2(w(E) dnu); the dnu pairing <K, f-g> of (A7) is handled by the explicit Fubini identity T2 (delta = f-g need not lie in L2(w dnu)). collCLM_adjoint_coeFn shows the Hilbert adjoint image coincides a.e. with collAdj K used in T4/T5.",
  "T9 instance: tau is any measurable bounded function of energy; the physical rate, medium and scatterer frame are not certified.",
  "CAS12-C01 finite adjoint is not imported and not used as evidence. No nonlinear, polarized or frequency-redistributing operator is defined."
 ],
